pithagoras-godot container image
Copyright 2026 Anirban Kar and Contributors

This image is what the Pithagoras Godot extension (@pithagoras/godot,
https://github.com/prometheion/pithagoras-extensions) runs. Its own files are under the Apache License 2.0:
/opt/godot-server/server.py and the editor plugin /opt/godot-server/addon/godot_mcp_bridge. The plugin is copied into
each Godot project the server opens, and is Apache-2.0 there too.

The image also contains, unchanged, software made by others, under their own licences. Their licence texts are in
this folder (/usr/share/doc/pithagoras-godot):

Godot Engine 4.7.2 (the editor and the web and linux export templates)
  MIT licence: godot-LICENSE.txt. The third-party code Godot contains, and its licences: godot-COPYRIGHT.txt.
  https://godotengine.org, source at https://github.com/godotengine/godot/tree/4.7.2-stable
  Not made or endorsed by the Godot Foundation.

Selkies 2.1.0 (the browser stream)
  Mozilla Public License 2.0: selkies-LICENSE. Its Python source is installed as it is, in /opt/selkies.
  Source at https://github.com/selkies-project/selkies/tree/2.1.0
  Selkies' .deb installs a Python environment of its own in /opt/selkies with about 30 third-party packages (aiohttp,
  cryptography, pillow, psutil, pylibsrtp, uvloop, watchdog and others). Each keeps its licence in its own
  /opt/selkies/lib/python3.12/site-packages/<package>-<version>.dist-info folder. Two of them are native code with
  libraries of their own bundled beside them: pixelflux and pcmflux, below.

pixelflux 2.2.1 (inside Selkies: screen capture and encoding)
  Mozilla Public License 2.0, with the licences of what it bundles: pixelflux-LICENSES.md. The build Selkies ships
  bundles libx264 (x264 commit b35605ace3ddf7c1a5d67a2eb553f034aef41d55, core 165) and x265 4.2 (commit
  e444744c03978c1fb4e037168967020cf2648427), which are under the GNU General Public License, version 2 or later;
  pixelflux's own notes say that the combined binary is then distributed under the GPL. Each release of this image on
  GitHub (https://github.com/prometheion/pithagoras-extensions/releases) carries the source of pixelflux 2.2.1 and of
  those x264 and x265 commits, as pixelflux's pyproject.toml pins them.
  pixelflux.libs also holds libde265 1.1.3 (commit ba62bf4cfb3242f3bf0a45617ff09e35236e4d82, under the GNU Lesser
  General Public License, version 3 or later, a separate shared library), and libvpx, dav1d and SVT-AV1 under BSD
  licences. Each release carries the libde265 source of that commit too.

pcmflux 2.2.0 (inside Selkies: audio capture; the stream's audio is off here, but the library is installed)
  Mozilla Public License 2.0: /opt/selkies/lib/python3.12/site-packages/pcmflux-2.2.0.dist-info/licenses/LICENSE, with
  the inventory of what it bundles, LICENSES.md, beside it, and the exact builds in its sboms/auditwheel.cdx.json.
  Source at https://github.com/selkies-project/pcmflux/tree/2.2.0. Its wheel bundles, as separate shared libraries in
  pcmflux.libs, libraries built by AlmaLinux 8, among them these under the GNU Lesser General Public License, version
  2.1 or later: libpulse and libpulsecommon (pulseaudio 14.0-4.el8), libasyncns (0.8-14.el8), libsndfile
  (1.0.28-17.el8_10), libsystemd (systemd 239-82.el8_10.19), libgcrypt (1.8.5-8.el8_10), libgpg-error (1.31-1.el8),
  libmount and libblkid (util-linux 2.32.1-48.el8_10). The others there are under permissive licences (libdbus-1 and
  libcap under the permissive one of their two, libFLAC, libvorbis, libogg, libuuid, libpcre2, liblz4, libgsm, liblzma,
  libselinux, libX11-xcb, libXi, libXtst), as LICENSES.md says. Each release carries pcmflux's source and the AlmaLinux
  source RPMs of those LGPL libraries.

VirtualGL 3.1.5 (OpenGL on the GPU for Compatibility-renderer projects)
  wxWindows Library Licence 3.1 (the GNU Library General Public Licence, version 2 or later, with an exception):
  virtualgl-LICENSE.txt, and the licence of the FLTK parts it carries: virtualgl-LICENSE-FLTK.txt. Its source tarball
  is attached to each release of this image too. https://virtualgl.org

Ubuntu 24.04 and the packages installed from its archive (among them Xvfb, Mesa, ffmpeg, xdotool, Python 3)
  Each under its own licence, in /usr/share/doc/<package>/copyright. Their sources are in Ubuntu's archive
  (https://archive.ubuntu.com, `apt-get source <package>`); each release of this image lists the packages and
  their versions (dpkg -l).

The NVIDIA driver is not in the image: the NVIDIA Container Toolkit puts the host's driver into the container when it
starts. The two loader manifests in /etc/vulkan/icd.d and /usr/share/glvnd/egl_vendor.d are a few lines written when
the image is built.
